Cilium L7 policy enforcement extends network security beyond IPs and ports. Using eBPF and Envoy, Cilium can inspect application-layer traffic such as HTTP, gRPC, Kafka, and DNS. Policies can allow or deny specific URLs, HTTP methods, headers, or API calls. Combined with Cilium identities derived from Kubernetes labels, security policies remain consistent even when pod IPs change, providing zero-trust microsegmentation for Kubernetes workloads.
Hubble is Cilium's observability platform. It leverages eBPF-generated flow data and provides visibility into pod-to-pod, pod-to-service, and pod-to-external communication. It shows which workloads are communicating, DNS queries, HTTP requests and responses, TCP connections, whether traffic is allowed or dropped, and helps validate Cilium Network Policies. It is widely used for troubleshooting, security monitoring, and network flow visualization in Kubernetes clusters
